    "knowledge_point_id": "0db14c2a-b6c3-5550-8ff6-c1a9e2b4ea5d",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given an Imaginary Root and Its Leading Coefficient",
    "problem": "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-\\,\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $5,$ find the equation.",
    "worked_solution": "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-\\,\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}})(x+\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{36}{5}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{36}{5} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $5.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({x}^{2}+\\frac{36}{5}) & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+36 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
